[Music]  hello my name is eleoni gomez alves i am  a biologist and i work for the max  planck institute for biogeochemistry  indiana germany i am florian whitman i  am professor at the khalsa institute of  technology  and i'm head of the department of  wetland ecology  and working for years in the ato project  [Music]  the amazon tao tower observatory atto is  a project from a collaboration between  brazil and germany and it has an  experimental site in the center of  amazon and this site we have a tao tower  a very tall tower of 325 meters and two  other towers of 80 meters height from  which we measured the air in the same  experimental site we also do research  with soils and vegetation  the main purpose of ato is to collect  information that tells us on  meteorological chemical and biological  processes that represent the forest the  atmosphere and how they talk to each  other the climate system is influenced  by this conversation between the forest  and the atmosphere and we at atom wanted  to disclose this conversation in order  to better understand natural processes  and also to be able to make more  accurate climate predictions  this started between the max planck  society specifically the max planck  institute for chemistry and minds  together with the national institute for  amazonian research empire in manaus  and  well we followed the idea and  found a place for the tower a good place  remote place  but not too remote  and  built it planned it built it during  several years  well the main question is how the  biosphere interacts with  the by the atmosphere  so  for instance which kinds of  gases  air results whatever  come out of the forest and how  increasingly changing atmospheres  interact  or influence forest growth and  the ecology and the functioning  of the forest so how this interchange  between forest and the atmosphere is  working  my research is on the volatile oregon  compounds emitted by plants  these compounds are known as the forest  scent  for you to have an idea of what i mean  with forest scent  try to remember when you did smell a  leaf of mint  i'm quite sure that you can recognize  this scent every time that you are  around mint  similarly the forest also has scents  which are blends of thousands and  thousands of compounds and there are  several reasons  why plants release these compounds  they can do so to communicate with other  plants what we call chemical  communication  or to repel  a predator for example some gases are  toxic to insects and once they are  released by a plant they can prevent an  insect attack to the plant  and when it's super hot or dry plants  can also release some of these gases to  survive  and after being released by plants these  gases get involved into chemical  reactions in the air that can transform  them into particles what we call  aerosols  this is a very important process in the  climate system because aerosols are  essential  for cloud formation when clouds are  being formed  it's necessary to have two things the  surface and the water vapor to condense  on the surface  and then start the process in which  clouds grow  aerosols are the surface  uh where the  water vapor condense on so they work as  a seed for cloud formation  my focus is to understand what triggers  plants to produce and release this sense  but as i am part of an interdisciplinary  team the results of my research can help  the atmospheric scientists to understand  the follow-up processes that these  scents take part in the atmosphere as  the formation of particles and then  cloud formation  what we have discovered is that this  blend of compounds  change  change substantially  over the seasons in central amazon we  basically have two seasons the rainy  season and the dry season and as i said  some of these compounds can protect  plants against heat  and drought  and during the dry season because  the the temperature  is higher and the air is drier we have  more emission of some compounds and this  is interesting because some of some  studies have predicted that in the  future we will have longer and more  intense dry seasons or droughts so this  can give us an indication that plants  probably will emit more of these  compounds to survive in these harsher  environments  my part  our part is more towards understanding  the ecology and eco-physiology of trees  so we are not directly involved in  atmospheric measurements but we are  involved in the functioning of trees for  instance  during  the season from where trees  take their water  if this is different between the  different rainforest ecosystems because  we have flooded forests we have dry  forests we have  tall rainforest and these forests are  quite different in species composition  and biodiversity  and functioning and our interest is  mainly focusing on how  these trees are able to track their  water sources  and if they are stressed out for  instance during dry conditions if there  are ecosystems that are more stressed  out than others  and also tree species that are more  stressed out during drought for instance  or during  inundations  [Music]  there are  hundreds of scientists involved in the  research there are the atmospheric  chemists  there are biologists there are  [Music]  people  from all over the globe from different  european countries from brazil  but also from the us  so  many many scientists involved in  different research questions going from  soils up to the atmosphere  the so-called boundary layer where we  have still the interchange between local  rainforest and the atmosphere closer to  the to the canopy  up to the height of 300 meters so there  are very different  scientific questions and scientists  involved  studying remote sensing  applying remote sensing applying  biological measurements  climate measurements and so on  and the logistics staff  which includes the drivers of cars and  boats  the staff that works in the maintenance  of the experimental side for example the  tower workers the cook  the locals from the communities around  ato these are people that help us in the  field with data collection  for example a tree climber from the  community works with me because he  collects the leaves from the tree top  that i use for my measurements  and also these locals help us because  they teach us many things about the  forest like species of plants and  animals  and also we have a lot of researchers  and students from local institutions  working at the experimental site  [Music]  auto is the biggest project in the  amazon forest that investigates the  interaction between forest and that in  the atmosphere that conversation that i  mentioned before  the long-term monitoring of gases like  co2 water vapor and the blend of plant  sands can give information that allows  us to see trends in the climate  for example uh we can investigate if the  forest is being a zinc or a source of  co2 the amazon forest has been  considered an important continental  carbon storage for the planet and in  fact the carbon stored in the amazon  forest represents 10 years of the  current global carbon emission and also  some past studies have suggested that  the forest is a sink of co2  from the atmosphere  this co2 sync role is a critical factor  in preventing faster rates of climate  change however  a recent study indicated that some  regions of the amazon forest are  becoming a source of co2 and these  together with other effects of climate  change like the  increase in temperature and more intense  and longer droughts can contribute to  the acceleration of climate change so  imagine if all of the carbon stored in  the amazon forest goes to the atmosphere  that would be really bad  so as the atop tower can measure gases  that come from very very far away as far  as 1 000 kilometer from the tower to the  east we can have information from a huge  area in the amazon and this can help us  to understand the interaction between  the amazon forest and the climate now  and in the future  first of course is the special situation  where we measure mostly pristine forests  with little influence of human impact  at a very special place in the middle of  the rainforest this is very unique on  the one hand and on the other hand  it brings together  joins all different kinds of researches  and institutions which of course work  interdisciplinary  at the international level and this of  course  important to respond broader questions  on on how for instance  rainforests may react to climate changes  how resilient these three species are  and how  resistant the entire ecosystem tropical  rainforest especially in the amazon  is  against the change actual  global climate change  [Music]  um you know that the rainforest is  important for  the entire human population not only for  the brazilians or for the europeans  it is  it is a very important source of carbon  and it is also a very important stock of  carbon and biomass and  of course  this  cooperation between germany and brazil  specifically the max planck society and  the brazilian institutions involved is  because we have long lasting  research programs  bilateral  programmes that last for more than  almost 50 years  so we have uh intense  interchange  of national interests here in  environmental research  which are the basis actually for the  success of that recent auto project  [Music]

